    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The change is implemented and the full test suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`).\n\n## What I did\n\nI added one small, self-contained `@ControllerAdvice` validator \u2014 `OwnerRegistrationDateValidationAdvice` \u2014 modeled exactly on the existing `OwnerPostcodeValidationAdvice`/`OwnerFieldsValidationAdvice` pattern in the same package. It registers itself via `@InitBinder` and rejects an `OwnerFieldsDto` whose supplied `registrationDate` is after `LocalDate.now()`, routing through the standard Bean Validation flow that the existing `ExceptionControllerAdvice` already turns into a **400**.\n\n## Why this shape\n\nPer the structural-impact cost, the cheapest move is to **add a new cohesive unit rather than modify any existing function**:\n\n- I changed **zero existing methods**, so no `WMC_other \u00d7 CC \u00d7 changed_lines` term is incurred for any pre-existing large class (e.g. the `Owner` model or `OwnerRestControllerV1`).\n- The new class has a single tiny `validate` method (CC \u2248 2) with essentially no surrounding class complexity, so its own context cost is minimal.\n- Validating the raw DTO (not the mapped `Owner`) also avoids interacting with `Owner.setRegistrationDate`'s business-day rolling logic, keeping the rule isolated and readable.\n\nThe acceptance test `errorRejectsFutureDate` (posts `registrationDate: \"2999-01-01\"`, expects 400) passes, and omitted/valid dates are untouched since the field remains optional.",
